---
import * as stylex from "@stylexjs/stylex";

import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, OPERATOR_NAME, PRIVACY_UPDATED } from "../site.ts";
import { styles } from "../styles.ts";

export const prerender = true;

const h2 = stylex.attrs(styles.heading);
const p = stylex.attrs(styles.text);
const ul = stylex.attrs(styles.list);
const li = stylex.attrs(styles.item);
const a = stylex.attrs(styles.link);
const mailto = `mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`;
---

<Page
  title="Privacy Policy | Voice"
  description="How the Voice Mac app, the Voice website, and Voice accounts handle your data."
  path="/privacy"
>
  <main {...stylex.attrs(styles.prose)}>
    <h1 {...stylex.attrs(styles.headline)}>Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p {...stylex.attrs(styles.updated)}>
      Last updated <time datetime={PRIVACY_UPDATED}>{PRIVACY_UPDATED}</time>
    </p>

    <p {...stylex.attrs(styles.lede)}>
      Voice transcribes your speech on your Mac. Your audio and your words do not leave your Mac.
    </p>

    <h2 {...h2}>Who we are</h2>
    <p {...p}>
      Voice is operated by {OPERATOR_NAME} ("we").
      You can reach us at <a href={mailto} {...a}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
    </p>
    <p {...p}>
      This policy covers the Voice Mac app, this website (voice.codlume.com), and Voice accounts.
    </p>

    <h2 {...h2}>What stays on your Mac</h2>
    <ul {...ul}>
      <li {...li}>
        <strong>Speech recognition.</strong> Voice recognizes speech locally, using the Parakeet model.
        Voice captures audio only while you hold the dictation shortcut. The audio is held in memory
        only. It is never written to disk and never sent anywhere. Voice discards it after
        transcription, or when you cancel.
      </li>
      <li {...li}>
        <strong>Cleanup.</strong> This optional step tidies up the transcribed text. It is on by
        default and works in English. It runs locally with a local model. Nothing is sent.
      </li>
      <li {...li}>
        <strong>Transcripts and files.</strong> Voice keeps the last transcript in memory until you
        quit Voice. It saves no history. Its settings file holds your microphone, shortcut, and
        preferences, and none of your text.
      </li>
      <li {...li}>
        <strong>Typing into apps.</strong> Voice types into the focused app through macOS
        Accessibility. It reads the contents of the focused field only to confirm that the text
        landed. What it reads stays in memory and is never stored or sent. If Voice has to paste, it
        uses the clipboard temporarily. Afterwards it restores your previous clipboard contents,
        unless something else changed the clipboard in the meantime. Voice never reads the name of
        the focused app, your screen, or your windows.
      </li>
      <li {...li}>
        <strong>Permissions.</strong> Voice asks for two macOS permissions. Microphone lets it hear
        you while you hold the shortcut. Accessibility lets it detect the shortcut and type text. The
        keyboard listener only looks for the shortcut. Your keystrokes are not recorded or sent.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 {...h2}>What the Mac app sends over the network</h2>
    <ul {...ul}>
      <li {...li}>
        <strong>Model downloads.</strong> Voice fetches the speech and cleanup models from Hugging
        Face (huggingface.co), and only when you click to download them. Hugging Face sees a normal
        download request, including your IP address. Voice sends no personal data from the app.
      </li>
      <li {...li}>
        <strong>Update checks.</strong> About 15 seconds after launch, and then every 4 hours, Voice
        requests the update feed from downloads.voice.codlume.com, which is hosted on Cloudflare. The
        request includes your release channel, your IP address, and a random identifier. The update
        library generates this identifier on its first check and stores it on your Mac. We do not
        use it to identify you. You can reset it by deleting the app's data.
      </li>
      <li {...li}>
        <strong>Diagnostics (opt-in, off by default).</strong> If you turn on sharing in Settings &gt;
        Data and Privacy, or during setup, Voice sends crash reports, dictation timings, and app
        events (such as a failed cleanup) to Sentry, hosted in the EU (Germany). Reports never
        include your words, your audio, your clipboard, or the app you dictate into. Voice removes
        error messages, and your Mac username in file paths, before sending. Turning sharing off
        stops sending immediately. Sentry receives your IP address as part of the connection. We
        configure Sentry not to store it. Sentry deletes reports after the retention period for our
        plan.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 {...h2}>Voice accounts</h2>
    <p {...p}>
      Accounts are optional and not yet available in every Voice build. You never have to sign in,
      and dictation works the same whether you are signed in or not.
    </p>
    <ul {...ul}>
      <li {...li}>
        <strong>Sign in with Google.</strong> Voice asks Google only for your name, email address,
        and profile picture (the openid, email, and profile scopes), plus Google's identifier for
        your account. We use them to create your account, show who is signed in, and recognize you
        when you sign in again. We do not request access to Gmail, Calendar, Drive, or any other
        Google data. We do not ask Google for offline access.
      </li>
      <li {...li}>
        <strong>Storage.</strong> We store your name, email address, Google account identifier, and
        profile picture URL in a Cloudflare D1 database located in Western Europe. We also store
        auth sessions and the tokens Google issues at sign-in. The access token is stored
        encrypted. The ID token, which holds the same profile details, is stored as Google issued
        it. Each auth session records the IP address and browser or app details (user agent)
        it was created from. On your Mac, Voice stores your auth session, and your name and email
        address so it can show who is signed in while offline, encrypted in its data folder. The
        encryption key is kept in the macOS Keychain. If encryption is unavailable, Voice keeps
        them only in memory, and you sign in again after restarting.
      </li>
      <li {...li}>
        <strong>Security records.</strong> To limit abuse, the account service keeps rate-limit
        records keyed by IP address.
      </li>
      <li {...li}>
        <strong>Signing out and deleting your account.</strong> Sign out in Settings &gt; Account
        ends the auth session on the server and removes it from your Mac. If the server cannot be
        reached, Voice keeps the old auth session encrypted on your Mac only to end it on the server
        when Voice next starts. Delete account on the same page removes your user record, your auth
        sessions, and your linked Google account record, including its stored Google tokens. If you
        signed in more than 10 minutes earlier, Voice first asks you to sign in again with the same
        Google account, and then ends the older auth session. We keep your account until you delete
        it.
      </li>
      <li {...li}>
        <strong>Nightly and Stable.</strong> Nightly and Stable builds keep separate accounts in
        separate databases. Signing in or deleting your account in one channel does not change your
        account in the other.
      </li>
      <li {...li}>
        <strong>What never reaches the account service.</strong> We never send audio, transcripts,
        clipboard contents, or app context to the account service.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p {...p}>
      We use Google user data only to provide sign-in. We do not sell it, use it for advertising, or
      use it to train models. We share it only with the service providers listed below.
    </p>

    <h2 {...h2}>The website</h2>
    <p {...p}>
      This website uses no cookies, no analytics, and no third-party scripts. Cloudflare serves the
      site. It processes your IP address and request details to deliver the site and protect it
      from abuse.
    </p>

    <h2 {...h2}>Service providers</h2>
    <ul {...ul}>
      <li {...li}>
        <strong>Cloudflare</strong> serves this website, the update downloads, and the account
        service. The account database is in Western Europe.
      </li>
      <li {...li}>
        <strong>Sentry</strong> receives diagnostics, only if you opt in. It is hosted in the EU.
      </li>
      <li {...li}>
        <strong>Google</strong> provides sign-in, only if you sign in.
      </li>
      <li {...li}>
        <strong>Hugging Face</strong> serves the model downloads, only when you click download.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p {...p}>We do not sell personal data or share it for advertising.</p>

    <h2 {...h2}>Legal bases</h2>
    <p {...p}>Under the GDPR, we rely on these legal bases:</p>
    <ul {...ul}>
      <li {...li}>
        <strong>Accounts.</strong> Performance of a contract. We need this data to provide the
        account you asked for.
      </li>
      <li {...li}>
        <strong>Diagnostics.</strong> Your consent. You can withdraw it at any time in Settings.
      </li>
      <li {...li}>
        <strong>Update checks, website delivery, and abuse protection.</strong> Our legitimate
        interests in keeping the app up to date and our services secure.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 {...h2}>International transfers</h2>
    <p {...p}>
      Some providers (Cloudflare, Google, and Hugging Face) may process data outside the European
      Economic Area. They rely on safeguards such as the EU Standard Contractual Clauses or the
      EU-US Data Privacy Framework.
    </p>

    <h2 {...h2}>Your rights</h2>
    <p {...p}>
      You have the right to access, correct, and delete your personal data. You can also restrict or
      object to its processing, and receive it in a portable format. Where we rely on your consent,
      you can withdraw it at any time.
    </p>
    <p {...p}>
      To exercise these rights, contact us at <a href={mailto} {...a}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>. You also
      have the right to complain to your local data protection authority.
    </p>

    <h2 {...h2}>Children</h2>
    <p {...p}>Voice accounts are not intended for children under 16.</p>

    <h2 {...h2}>Changes</h2>
    <p {...p}>
      When we change this policy, we update this page and the "Last updated" date. We announce
      significant changes in the app or in the release notes.
    </p>
  </main>
</Page>
